      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "@{68098a4626271974ef4d2775|full_name} What if the user's use case is running VRChat on Meta Quest and run the OSC tool on another device? Installing an extra app on Quest is not simple unless you upload the app to Meta Store. Also, running another app (that at least has to track file changes, and either process them or send them over the network, or both) would consume resources and Quest is very limited in that matter. And also, there's no guarantee that the log format or API won't change.\n\nBtw I'm working on an OSC avatar scaling tool and having these over OSC would be very convenient to implement some (niche) features. I think other OSC tool creators could find some uses too.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2026-10-07T18:12:04.695Z",
  "deleteID": null,
  "deletedAt": null,
  "deletedBy": null,
  "details": "Instance: id, name, type (public/invite/etc.), owner, status, player count, capacity, restrictions (age-gate and blocked features). World: id, name, tags, which favorite list it is in (if it is favorited by the user).\n\nFor privacy, some of these might be behind a setting (like the head and wrist tracking data). Additionally, you can make the setting only show up when requested via OSCQuery to avoid bloating the settings menu.\n\nThese are possible to get from the logs and the API but you need to run a program on the same device or have to handle auth. Sending these over OSC would make making some cross-platform and cross-device OSC programs simpler, and more convenient for users to use.\n\nFor me, world id and instance type are the most important.",
